mobile.patch: run vl-convert on QuickJS instead of V8.

vl-convert executes Vega and Vega-Lite inside Deno. Deno's default engine is V8,
which has no Android build at all and only jitless arm64 iOS builds, and whose
startup snapshot (made at build time by a host V8) cannot be loaded by a V8
built for another configuration. deno_core 0.411 can instead run on QuickJS-ng
through its deno_v8 facade (the `quickjs` feature, backed by the v8x crate): a
plain C interpreter that cross-compiles like any -sys crate and needs no JIT.

1. Cargo.toml: deno_core with `quickjs` in place of its default `v8`.
   pyo3 without `abi3-py310`: an abi3 cross build links the abi3 floor,
   libpython3.10, which the iOS link cannot find. forge builds one wheel per
   Python anyway.

2. vl-convert-rs: boot without a snapshot. QuickJS cannot load a V8 snapshot,
   so build.rs is swapped for forge_build.rs, which writes the empty snapshot
   and residual-source tables lib.rs includes; the converter passes
   `startup_snapshot: None`, and deno_runtime gets `transpile` so it can strip
   the TypeScript in its extension sources at startup (deno_core.patch and
   deno_runtime.patch embed those sources, which Deno otherwise only reads from
   build-machine paths while snapshotting). Dropping build.rs also drops its
   host-side deno_runtime build and V8 download.

3. vl-convert-rs: aws-lc-sys 0.40 (pulled in by Deno's crypto crates) ships
   pregenerated bindings for Android arm64 only; enable its `bindgen` feature
   for every mobile target.

4. .cargo/config.toml + .cargo/cross.cmake: v8x builds WAMR (QuickJS's
   WebAssembly backend) with a bare `cmake` call that would configure for the
   build host, and aws-lc-sys's bindgen mode (item 3) uses its CMake builder,
   which skips its own cross setup whenever CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE is set. CMake
   reads that variable from the environment; cargo's [env] sets it for build
   scripts, and the toolchain file keys off the TARGET triple cargo gives them,
   so host builds are unaffected.

5. vl-convert-google-fonts: a MobileTls builder extension, applied to every
   reqwest client in both crates. reqwest's rustls backend verifies through
   rustls-platform-verifier, which panics on Android unless initialised from
   Java with a JNI context; on Android the clients trust the bundled Mozilla
   roots (webpki-root-certs) instead. Other targets are unchanged.

6. worker_pool.rs: when a worker thread dies before reporting startup, put its
   panic message in the error instead of "receiving on a closed channel". On
   Android a Rust panic's own output goes to a discarded stderr.

7. vl-convert-python/forge_tls.c (Android only): Rust has no native TLS on
   Android, so std backs every thread_local! with its own pthread key, and
   bionic allows a process about 128. A worker touches about 80, so the next
   native module to load (rpds-py, under Altair) aborted with "out of TLS keys".
   Hidden definitions of the four pthread key functions bind every reference in
   this .so and multiplex its keys onto one real key.

diff -ruN a/.cargo/config.toml b/.cargo/config.toml
--- a/.cargo/config.toml
+++ b/.cargo/config.toml
@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@
+[env]
+CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE = { value = ".cargo/cross.cmake", relative = true }
diff -ruN a/.cargo/cross.cmake b/.cargo/cross.cmake
--- a/.cargo/cross.cmake
+++ b/.cargo/cross.cmake
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# Cross toolchain for CMake projects driven by a Rust build script. v8x builds
+# WAMR with a bare `cmake` call, which would otherwise configure for the build
+# host, and aws-lc-sys's bindgen mode uses its CMake builder, which skips its own
+# cross setup when CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE is set. $ENV{TARGET} is the Rust target
+# triple cargo gives the build script; host-side builds match neither branch.
+set(_forge_target "$ENV{TARGET}")
+
+if(_forge_target MATCHES "-linux-android")
+  if(_forge_target MATCHES "^aarch64-")
+    set(ANDROID_ABI arm64-v8a)
+  elseif(_forge_target MATCHES "^x86_64-")
+    set(ANDROID_ABI x86_64)
+  elseif(_forge_target MATCHES "^i686-")
+    set(ANDROID_ABI x86)
+  else()
+    set(ANDROID_ABI armeabi-v7a)
+  endif()
+  if(DEFINED ENV{ANDROID_API_LEVEL})
+    set(ANDROID_PLATFORM "android-$ENV{ANDROID_API_LEVEL}")
+  else()
+    set(ANDROID_PLATFORM android-24)
+  endif()
+  set(WAMR_BUILD_PLATFORM android CACHE STRING "")
+  include("$ENV{NDK_ROOT}/build/cmake/android.toolchain.cmake")
+elseif(_forge_target MATCHES "-apple-ios")
+  set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME iOS)
+  if(_forge_target MATCHES "-sim$" OR _forge_target MATCHES "^x86_64-")
+    set(CMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT iphonesimulator)
+  else()
+    set(CMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT iphoneos)
+  endif()
+  if(_forge_target MATCHES "^aarch64-")
+    set(CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES arm64)
+  else()
+    set(CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES x86_64)
+  endif()
+  set(CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET 13.0)
+  set(WAMR_BUILD_PLATFORM darwin CACHE STRING "")
+endif()
